===Etymology 3===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₂enk-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|angel|t=fishhook}}, from {{inh|en|ang|angel|t=hook, fishhook}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*angul}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*angulaz|t=hook}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*h₂enk-|t=to make crooked, bend}}. Cognate with {{cog|fy|angel|t=fishing rod, stinger}}, {{cog|nl|angel|t=fishhook}}, {{cog|de|Angel|t=fishing pole}}, {{cog|is|öngull|t=fishhook}}, {{cog|prn|uku|t=shoulder}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# A [[fishhook]]; [[tackle]] for catching fish, consisting of a line, hook, and bait, with or without a rod.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Antony and Cleopatra|II|v|passage=Give me mine '''angle''': we'll to the river there.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1717|author=w:Alexander Pope|title=Vertuminus and Pomona
|passage=A fisher next his trembling '''angle''' bears.}}

===Etymology 4===
From {{inh|en|enm|anglen|t=to fish, fish with a hook|lit=to fish-hook}}, perhaps from {{inh|en|ang|*anglian}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*anglōn|t=to hook}}. Cognate with {{cog|stq|ongelje|t=to fish, angle}}, {{cog|nl|hengelen|t=to fish, angle}}, {{cog|nds-de|angeln|t=to fish, angle}}, {{cog|de|angeln|t=to fish, angle}}.

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To try to [[catch]] [[fish]] with a [[hook]] and [[line]].
# {{lb|en|figurative|informal|with [[for]]}} To attempt to subtly persuade someone to offer a desired thing.
#: {{ux|en|He must be '''angling''' for a pay rise.}}

